    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "E-cigarettes, devices that heat solutions of nicotine and many other chemicals, have a myriad of\r\nnegative physical and mental health effects that are especially harmful to adolescents and young\r\nadults (AYA). E-cigarette use among AYA also remains high in the 2020s. Some solutions to this\r\nwidespread problem that have gained traction are e-cigarette cessation programs and education\r\nprograms, which respectively seek to support AYA in quitting e-cigarettes and give them\r\ninformation through either marketing programs or videos on e-cigarette use created by health\r\norganizations or students. Also important to consider are the aesthetics of e-cigarette education\r\nprograms, which have a notable effect on the social norms and decisions of AYA and have not\r\nbeen reviewed in the past.\r\nThis literature review analyzes the extent to which e-cigarette cessation and prevention programs\r\nare efficacious in promoting e-cigarette cessation or knowledge. It also examines the\r\neffectiveness of e-cigarette education programs in appealing to AYA and compares a student-led\r\ne-cigarette education program to an education program created by a federal organization to\r\ndetermine the efficacy of different education programs. Finally, this review gives\r\nrecommendations for both future e-cigarette cessation programs and education programs, and for\r\nresearch in this field.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chen, B. (2024). Texts, Pictures, Videos: A Literature Review of E-Cigarette Cessation and Prevention Programs. Version 1. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wv714cf7041/version/1. https://doi.org/10.25740/wv714cf7041.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
